WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.857 --> 00:00:04.886
A grid operator wants to connect small business customers faster.

2
00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:10.251
A stakeholder is anyone who affects that project, or is affected by it.

3
00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.428
Four roles matter.

4
00:00:13.127 --> 00:00:15.275
The sponsor owns budget and result,

5
00:00:15.496 --> 00:00:18.735
and decides: the director of customer connections.

6
00:00:19.743 --> 00:00:23.214
The process owner runs the process and lives with the change:

7
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:24.886
the head of work preparation.

8
00:00:25.603 --> 00:00:29.759
Users, whose work changes: work planners and technicians.

9
00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:34.373
Blockers: anyone whose interest, power or time is hit.

10
00:00:35.187 --> 00:00:39.576
Often IT, the works council, or a shift lead with twenty years in.

11
00:00:40.911 --> 00:00:42.583
Put them on a stakeholder map.

12
00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.699
Influence: can they stop it or speed it up?

13
00:00:46.409 --> 00:00:48.371
Interest: does it change their work?

14
00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:51.518
High on both: involve weekly.

15
00:00:52.196 --> 00:00:57.572
High influence, low interest: keep satisfied, short updates in their terms.

16
00:00:58.311 --> 00:01:02.328
The reverse: keep informed and let them join, they know the details.

17
00:01:03.339 --> 00:01:08.378
Influential and quiet: the IT owner and the team leads who assign crews.

18
00:01:09.128 --> 00:01:12.669
Nobody invited them: book three one-on-ones in week one.

19
00:01:13.669 --> 00:01:18.034
Draw it on paper after the intake and update it monthly: people move.

20
00:01:19.335 --> 00:01:22.168
Just ask: who decides whether this goes ahead?

21
00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:25.327
Who could stop it, even without saying so?

22
00:01:26.039 --> 00:01:27.502
And who has done this before?

23
00:01:28.542 --> 00:01:33.302
The second often gets a laugh and a name: see that person one-on-one this week.

24
00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:38.858
So you learn crews get pulled to faults 30 percent of the time: key data.

25
00:01:39.952 --> 00:01:44.341
Check the works council early: it must consent to new working times,

26
00:01:44.561 --> 00:01:48.067
like shift patterns, or the project can stall for months.

27
00:01:49.452 --> 00:01:53.806
Resistance rarely sounds like no: missed meetings, people who aren't freed up,

28
00:01:53.992 --> 00:01:56.488
or: let's discuss it in the management team.

29
00:01:57.476 --> 00:02:02.654
Escalation, taking a blocked issue to someone higher up who can decide, comes last.

30
00:02:03.403 --> 00:02:06.932
First ask: what would this need to look like to work for your team?

31
00:02:07.915 --> 00:02:11.248
Only when a decision is truly blocked, go to the sponsor,

32
00:02:11.445 --> 00:02:13.221
and tell the person beforehand.

33
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.770
A typical failure: only names from the org chart.

34
00:02:18.524 --> 00:02:23.853
The real power often sits with a planner everyone asks for advice, or the union rep.

35
00:02:24.874 --> 00:02:26.232
Show the map to the client?

36
00:02:27.005 --> 00:02:28.166
Experts argue.

37
00:02:28.883 --> 00:02:33.028
Many keep a neutral version for the team, and their own notes private.

38
00:02:34.101 --> 00:02:37.398
Ask the sponsor: whose support do we need that we don't have yet?

39
00:02:38.759 --> 00:02:40.547
You can't give orders: you trade.

40
00:02:41.325 --> 00:02:45.830
What you offer is currency: help, information, visibility, time saved,

41
00:02:46.039 --> 00:02:47.409
a problem off their plate.

42
00:02:48.455 --> 00:02:52.693
People repay what they receive, so give first: data someone wants,

43
00:02:52.843 --> 00:02:55.700
or a finding before it goes to the steering committee:

44
00:02:55.885 --> 00:02:58.196
the senior people who oversee your project,

45
00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.087
often monthly, and take its big decisions.

46
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.190
Credibility, even as the youngest,

47
00:03:04.352 --> 00:03:08.207
doesn't come from a title: your numbers are right, you do what you said,

48
00:03:08.381 --> 00:03:10.378
when you said, and you are on the floor.

49
00:03:11.446 --> 00:03:14.361
When it's missing, you hear: interesting, let's see.

50
00:03:15.082 --> 00:03:16.371
Then nothing happens.

51
00:03:17.708 --> 00:03:22.445
Map before you move: this stakeholder map puts everyone on influence and stance,

52
00:03:22.514 --> 00:03:23.594
from against to for.

53
00:03:24.353 --> 00:03:26.002
Tuesday: your readiness check.

54
00:03:27.016 --> 00:03:30.186
First win Ans, a work preparer: influential,

55
00:03:30.314 --> 00:03:32.706
still neutral, and others listen to her.

56
00:03:33.423 --> 00:03:38.218
Show her the data, let her poke holes, and visibly adopt her best fix.

57
00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:42.274
Then the sceptical head of execution, one-on-one.

58
00:03:42.978 --> 00:03:45.555
His currency: fewer wasted crew trips.

59
00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:48.727
Ask: what would have to be in this for you to back it?

60
00:03:49.756 --> 00:03:52.206
Keep the sponsor, who commissioned the project,

61
00:03:52.392 --> 00:03:54.830
in reserve: each call spends goodwill.

62
00:03:55.527 --> 00:03:58.639
On Tuesday, Ans explains what she improved.

63
00:03:59.666 --> 00:04:04.844
Talk one-on-one before any group meeting: nobody likes a surprise in front of peers.

64
00:04:05.535 --> 00:04:07.776
Ask: what would make this worth it for you?

65
00:04:09.093 --> 00:04:12.042
A cable manufacturer wants better weekly planning.

66
00:04:12.731 --> 00:04:16.341
You draft a RACI: roles per deliverable, in four letters.

67
00:04:17.355 --> 00:04:18.887
Responsible does the work.

68
00:04:19.702 --> 00:04:22.848
Accountable owns the result and says: this is done.

69
00:04:23.577 --> 00:04:25.017
Exactly one person.

70
00:04:26.044 --> 00:04:29.191
Consulted gives input before, in a two-way talk.

71
00:04:29.941 --> 00:04:32.066
Informed hears after, one way.

72
00:04:32.777 --> 00:04:35.308
Two names under A: nobody owns it.

73
00:04:36.362 --> 00:04:40.646
For the capacity model, hours per machine per week: R you, A the planner.

74
00:04:41.661 --> 00:04:46.433
One owner per deliverable, from the client side, who runs it after you leave.

75
00:04:47.152 --> 00:04:50.833
If the client owns nothing, they only watch: a red flag.

76
00:04:52.134 --> 00:04:57.126
The client's senior sponsor backs the project, decides and clears obstacles.

77
00:04:57.856 --> 00:05:01.583
Agree in writing: chairs the steering group every four weeks,

78
00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:07.168
decides scope within a week, opens the talk with sales, tells the shift leads why.

79
00:05:08.179 --> 00:05:10.431
Two managers both claim the shift pattern?

80
00:05:11.148 --> 00:05:13.331
Ask: who decides if you disagree?

81
00:05:14.024 --> 00:05:15.324
Otherwise the sponsor.

82
00:05:16.035 --> 00:05:17.997
Better week 2 than week 12.

83
00:05:19.007 --> 00:05:23.175
Failures: made alone, too many Cs, or filed and forgotten.

84
00:05:23.904 --> 00:05:27.526
RASCI or DACI: the conversation counts more than the letters.

85
00:05:28.551 --> 00:05:31.964
A sharp question: if this goes wrong, whose phone rings?
